# Git Workflow

All Git operations in this repository must follow GitHub Flow and the
conventions defined below.

## GitHub Flow

- Treat `main` as the stable, deployable branch.
- Never commit directly to `main`.
- Create one short-lived branch for each logical change.
- Create new branches from an up-to-date `main`, unless the repository
  documentation explicitly defines another base.
- Open a pull request, run the relevant checks, and request review before
  merging.
- Delete the branch after it is merged when it is no longer needed.

## Branches

Use this pattern:

```text
<type>/<short-description>
```

Allowed branch types:

- `feat`: new functionality
- `fix`: bug fix
- `refactor`: code restructuring without a behavior change
- `chore`: maintenance or configuration
- `docs`: documentation only
- `test`: test additions or corrections
- `perf`: performance improvement
- `build`: build system or dependency changes
- `ci`: continuous integration or delivery changes

Branch names must:

- be lowercase
- use kebab-case
- be short and descriptive
- describe one logical change
- contain no spaces, underscores, accents, or uppercase letters

Examples for this project:

```text
feat/group-invitations
feat/checkin-comments
fix/daily-reminder-timezone
refactor/ranking-cache
chore/update-dependencies
docs/update-supabase-setup
test/checkin-validation
perf/feed-loading
ci/e2e-checks
```

## Commits

Use Conventional Commits with a mandatory scope:

```text
<type>(<scope>): <description>
```

Allowed commit types:

- `feat`
- `fix`
- `refactor`
- `perf`
- `style`
- `test`
- `docs`
- `chore`
- `build`
- `ci`
- `revert`

Commit messages must:

- include a scope
- use a lowercase scope
- use a short noun for the affected domain, module, or infrastructure
- use a concise English description
- start the description with a lowercase word
- describe what the commit does
- not end the description with a period
- represent one logical change

Useful scopes for this project include:

```text
auth
groups
checkins
feed
ranking
profile
notifications
reminders
push
pwa
api
database
supabase
ui
deps
config
github
ci
docs
```

Choose the narrowest meaningful scope. A scope outside this list is allowed
when it describes the affected area more accurately.

Examples:

```text
feat(groups): add invitation link sharing
feat(checkins): add image upload
fix(reminders): respect the user timezone
fix(auth): preserve the password reset session
refactor(ranking): extract cache invalidation
perf(feed): reduce initial query size
test(checkins): cover weekly activity limits
docs(readme): clarify Supabase setup
chore(deps): update project dependencies
ci(github): add pull request checks
```

Do not use vague messages such as:

```text
changes
adjustments
update
fix stuff
final
working
small changes
```

## Commit granularity

Each commit must contain one logical change. Split unrelated changes into
separate commits, even when they belong to the same branch.

Prefer:

```text
feat(checkins): add activity selector
fix(checkins): reject duplicate daily entries
test(checkins): cover duplicate entry validation
```

Instead of:

```text
feat(checkins): add selector and fix validation and tests
```

## Breaking changes

Add `!` before the colon when a change breaks an existing public contract:

```text
feat(api)!: change the checkin response format
```

Include a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer when migration details or a longer
explanation are necessary.

## Agent instructions

When asked to create a branch:

1. Inspect the current branch and working tree when necessary.
2. Determine the correct branch type from the actual task.
3. Generate a lowercase kebab-case name using `<type>/<short-description>`.
4. Show the proposed name before or while creating the branch.
5. Do not switch branches when uncommitted changes could be overwritten or
   mixed with unrelated work.

When asked to create commits:

1. Inspect `git status` and the relevant staged and unstaged diffs.
2. Understand the purpose of each change before selecting a message.
3. Separate unrelated changes into distinct commits.
4. Select the correct type and the narrowest meaningful scope.
5. Use `<type>(<scope>): <description>` for every commit.
6. Run checks appropriate to the changes before reporting completion.

Never create a commit outside this convention.
